# SECTION 5 — REAL-TIME VISITOR MANAGEMENT

Rebuild the Visitor Management Module into a functional, role-based visitor authorization workflow.

This is one of the highest-priority modules.

## 5.1 Security Staff visitor registration

Security Staff must be able to:

- Register a new visitor.
- Enter visitor name.
- Enter mobile number where required.
- Enter visitor type or purpose.
- Enter the destination flat number.
- Select the correct wing or building where applicable.
- Enter the expected visit details.
- Capture or upload a visitor photograph using a supported interface.
- Submit the visitor permission request.
- View the request status.
- Record entry after authorization.
- Record exit when the visitor leaves.
- View permitted visitor records and history.

Validate visitor information before submission.

If camera capture is supported, request the necessary browser or device permission. Provide a secure photo-upload fallback where appropriate.

Store photographs in private storage. Do not expose visitor images through public URLs.

## 5.2 Permission routing

When Security Staff submits visitor details, the request must be routed according to the selected destination flat number.

The system must:

1. Identify the correct flat.
2. Identify the resident or owner associated with that flat using the existing data model.
3. Identify the authorized Admin account or review queue.
4. Save the visitor request.
5. Notify the appropriate Resident and Admin.
6. Display the same request status consistently across their authorized interfaces.

Avoid sending requests to unrelated residents or displaying them to the wrong flat owner.

If the existing application has separate owner, tenant, and resident relationships, follow its established rules to identify who is authorized to approve a visitor.

## 5.3 Resident approval interface

Residents must be able to:

- View incoming visitor requests for their own flat.
- View visitor photographs and details.
- Approve a request.
- Reject a request.
- See pending, approved, and rejected requests.
- View their own visitor history.
- See entry and exit information where authorized.

Residents must not be able to approve or reject visitor requests for another flat.

## 5.4 Admin approval interface

The Admin must be able to:

- View visitor requests across the society.
- View the visitor's photograph and submitted details.
- View the destination flat and assigned resident.
- Approve requests.
- Reject requests.
- Record rejection reasons.
- Monitor pending requests.
- View visitor entry and exit history.
- Search and filter visitor records.

The Admin and concerned Resident must both have the requested approval and rejection options, subject to their assigned permissions.

Maintain a single authoritative visitor request record so decisions cannot silently contradict each other.

If either authorized reviewer makes a decision, synchronize the status across the system. If the Resident and Admin submit conflicting decisions concurrently, apply a server-side rule that prevents inconsistent final states and records the conflict for review.

## 5.5 Real-time functionality

Implement actual real-time or near-real-time updates using the existing backend's supported capabilities.

Requirements:

- Update request status after approval or rejection.
- Update the Security Staff interface when a decision is received.
- Notify the concerned parties when a request changes status.
- Update visitor lists without requiring repeated manual refreshes when supported.
- Use database-backed state as the source of truth.
- Handle connection loss and retry or refresh safely.
- Do not simulate real-time functionality using fake data.

If Supabase Realtime is already used, integrate with the existing configuration instead of introducing an unnecessary second system.

## 5.6 Visitor authorization rules

- Security Staff cannot approve their own visitor permission requests.
- Security Staff must check the final permission status before recording an authorized entry.
- Rejected visitors must not be marked as authorized.
- Record the actual entry and exit timestamps.
- Prevent duplicate active entry records for the same visit.
- Preserve the decision history and reviewer identity.
- Provide an appropriate workflow for emergency or exceptional access only if an authorized, documented society policy exists.
- Do not automatically mark a visitor as approved simply because a request was submitted.

